WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.285
Lesson 4.6.

00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:04.955
Airdrops & points: opportunity cost.

00:00:04.955 --> 00:00:07.797
By the end, you'll be able to price farming

00:00:07.797 --> 00:00:10.639
for a future airdrop as exactly what it is:

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:13.740
a bet, with real costs, not a guaranteed reward.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.330
Here's the plan.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:18.594
How airdrops and points actually work,

00:00:18.594 --> 00:00:20.635
and who decides who gets what.

00:00:20.635 --> 00:00:23.107
Why nothing here is ever promised, and the

00:00:23.107 --> 00:00:26.110
scam risk that follows directly from that fact.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.815
A full worked example, pricing a specific

00:00:28.815 --> 00:00:31.240
farm as an actual expected value.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.015
And a checklist for deciding whether farming for

00:00:34.015 --> 00:00:36.280
one is even worth your time and capital.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:38.995
How this actually works.

00:00:40.295 --> 00:00:43.400
Here's the actual sequence most airdrops follow.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.937
A protocol wants to reward its earliest, genuine users,

00:00:46.937 --> 00:00:50.900
specifically with tokens, once it eventually launches one.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.198
Long before that token exists, it very often tracks

00:00:54.198 --> 00:00:58.685
your activity as points instead, a running, unofficial scoreboard.

00:00:58.685 --> 00:01:01.987
Eligibility rules, and a specific snapshot moment

00:01:01.987 --> 00:01:04.995
in time, decide who ultimately qualifies.

00:01:04.995 --> 00:01:08.379
And sybil filters are specifically designed to exclude

00:01:08.379 --> 00:01:11.394
anyone believed to have split into many wallets,

00:01:11.394 --> 00:01:14.163
purely to farm a larger share for themselves.

00:01:15.463 --> 00:01:17.816
Here's that same sequence, laid out as

00:01:17.816 --> 00:01:20.228
the actual stages it moves through.

00:01:20.228 --> 00:01:22.120
You take real actions, using

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.704
a protocol, genuinely or otherwise.

00:01:24.704 --> 00:01:27.526
The protocol tracks that activity, often as

00:01:27.526 --> 00:01:30.199
points, well before any token exists.

00:01:30.199 --> 00:01:34.604
At some undisclosed snapshot moment, eligibility gets locked in.

00:01:34.604 --> 00:01:36.952
Sybil filters then attempt to exclude

00:01:36.952 --> 00:01:39.724
wallets believed to be farming unfairly.

00:01:39.724 --> 00:01:41.670
And only after all of that does

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.798
an actual token, if one ever comes

00:01:43.798 --> 00:01:45.744
at all, finally get distributed.

00:01:47.045 --> 00:01:49.756
Worth being precise about what a sybil filter

00:01:49.756 --> 00:01:52.526
is actually trying to catch, since it directly

00:01:52.526 --> 00:01:55.125
shapes how these programs get designed.

00:01:55.125 --> 00:01:58.344
It's specifically trying to exclude wallets believed to

00:01:58.344 --> 00:02:01.391
belong to one person splitting themselves into many,

00:02:01.391 --> 00:02:04.565
purely to claim a disproportionately larger share.

00:02:04.565 --> 00:02:08.071
That's exactly why protocols keep both their eligibility

00:02:08.071 --> 00:02:11.638
rules and their snapshot timing deliberately vague, right

00:02:11.638 --> 00:02:14.405
up until the moment they're finally revealed.

00:02:15.705 --> 00:02:18.548
Worth being precise about why protocols actually

00:02:18.548 --> 00:02:20.637
run these programs at all, since it

00:02:20.637 --> 00:02:23.490
clarifies what they genuinely want from you.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:27.522
They're trying to reward genuine early users specifically, and

00:02:27.522 --> 00:02:31.810
distribute ownership more broadly, rather than concentrating a new

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.310
token entirely among insiders and early investors.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.283
That underlying goal is exactly why sybil filters exist,

00:02:39.283 --> 00:02:42.838
and exactly why genuine, organic usage tends to be

00:02:42.838 --> 00:02:47.299
rewarded over obviously artificial, repetitive farming patterns.

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:49.689
Quick check.

00:02:49.689 --> 00:02:51.534
What exactly is a sybil filter?

00:02:55.954 --> 00:02:59.120
The answer: rules that exclude wallets believed to

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.851
belong to the same person, farming the same

00:03:01.851 --> 00:03:04.893
program many times over through multiple wallets.

00:03:06.193 --> 00:03:07.898
Nothing here is ever promised.

00:03:09.198 --> 00:03:12.605
Worth being completely precise about this, since it's the

00:03:12.605 --> 00:03:15.783
single most important fact in this entire lesson.

00:03:15.783 --> 00:03:18.543
Points are not a promise of a future token.

00:03:18.543 --> 00:03:21.190
There is no contract, no guarantee, and no

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:24.453
obligation on the protocol's part to ever distribute

00:03:24.453 --> 00:03:27.100
anything at all, no matter how many points

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.856
you've accumulated, or how confident the community narrative

00:03:30.856 --> 00:03:32.518
around it happens to sound.

00:03:33.818 --> 00:03:37.331
Here's exactly why that uncertainty becomes the scammer's

00:03:37.331 --> 00:03:40.481
opening, since it's a direct consequence of nothing

00:03:40.481 --> 00:03:43.810
being promised or officially confirmed in advance.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:48.000
Real anticipation, and real uncertainty about timing, builds

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.665
up around a genuinely large, unconfirmed airdrop.

00:03:51.665 --> 00:03:54.672
A fake claim site then appears, closely mimicking

00:03:54.672 --> 00:03:57.378
the real protocol, right at the exact moment

00:03:57.378 --> 00:04:01.045
people are anxiously watching for the real announcement.

00:04:01.045 --> 00:04:03.803
And anyone who connects their wallet, or signs

00:04:03.803 --> 00:04:06.679
what looks like a routine claim transaction, can

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:09.085
lose everything in that wallet instantly.

00:04:10.384 --> 00:04:12.834
Worth repeating the one rule that defeats

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:15.692
this specific scam completely, since it's simple

00:04:15.692 --> 00:04:18.559
enough to actually follow every single time.

00:04:18.559 --> 00:04:21.307
Only ever claim from a link you've verified

00:04:21.307 --> 00:04:25.117
through the protocol's own official channel, directly, never

00:04:25.117 --> 00:04:27.303
from a link in a random message, a

00:04:27.303 --> 00:04:29.738
comment, or a search ad, no matter how

00:04:29.738 --> 00:04:32.174
urgent or time-limited it claims to be.

00:04:33.474 --> 00:04:34.564
Quick check.

00:04:34.564 --> 00:04:36.824
Are points a promise of a future token?

00:04:41.244 --> 00:04:44.211
The answer: no. Never treat a points balance as

00:04:44.211 --> 00:04:47.239
a promise, a receipt, or a guarantee of anything.

00:04:48.539 --> 00:04:49.544
Worked example.

00:04:50.844 --> 00:04:53.429
Here's the expected-value formula first,

00:04:53.429 --> 00:04:55.999
using this program's own calculator.

00:04:55.999 --> 00:05:00.298
Probability of actually qualifying, times the airdrop's estimated

00:05:00.298 --> 00:05:03.163
value, minus your real costs of farming it,

00:05:03.163 --> 00:05:06.329
gas, time, and everything else you've spent.

00:05:06.329 --> 00:05:09.296
Plug in a thirty percent chance of qualifying,

00:05:09.296 --> 00:05:12.515
an estimated fifteen hundred dollar value, and two

00:05:12.515 --> 00:05:15.545
hundred dollars of real costs, and the expected

00:05:15.545 --> 00:05:18.638
value comes to exactly two hundred fifty dollars.

00:05:19.939 --> 00:05:23.194
Now here's the actual comparison this expected value

00:05:23.194 --> 00:05:26.080
has to survive, against what that same capital

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.959
could have earned somewhere else entirely.

00:05:28.959 --> 00:05:31.507
If the ten thousand dollars tied up farming

00:05:31.507 --> 00:05:34.519
this airdrop could instead have earned a risk-light

00:05:34.519 --> 00:05:37.647
five percent elsewhere, that's five hundred dollars a

00:05:37.647 --> 00:05:40.669
year, in an ordinary, well-understood position.

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:43.938
The farm's two hundred fifty dollar expected value

00:05:43.938 --> 00:05:46.758
has to beat that five hundred dollars, plus

00:05:46.758 --> 00:05:49.450
compensate you for its own extra risk, or

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:51.309
it's simply the worse choice.

00:05:52.609 --> 00:05:55.014
How much does the guess for probability matter?

00:05:56.314 --> 00:05:59.612
Here's that same fifteen hundred dollar value and two

00:05:59.612 --> 00:06:03.215
hundred dollar cost, run across five different guesses for

00:06:03.215 --> 00:06:06.879
your actual probability of qualifying, since that number is

00:06:06.879 --> 00:06:10.599
almost always the shakiest input in this entire formula.

00:06:10.599 --> 00:06:14.396
At ten percent, the expected value is actually negative,

00:06:14.396 --> 00:06:18.359
fifty dollars, meaning the farm loses money on average.

00:06:18.359 --> 00:06:20.011
At twenty percent, it turns

00:06:20.011 --> 00:06:22.259
positive, at one hundred dollars.

00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:23.849
At the source material's own

00:06:23.849 --> 00:06:26.014
thirty percent, two hundred fifty.

00:06:26.014 --> 00:06:28.489
And at fifty percent, five hundred fifty.

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:32.693
Notice what that negative value at ten percent

00:06:32.693 --> 00:06:35.411
actually tells you, since it's easy to only

00:06:35.411 --> 00:06:38.244
picture the optimistic end of this range.

00:06:38.244 --> 00:06:41.064
If your real odds of qualifying are genuinely

00:06:41.064 --> 00:06:43.946
that low, the honest expected value of farming

00:06:43.946 --> 00:06:46.705
is a loss, on average, even before comparing

00:06:46.705 --> 00:06:49.463
it against anything else you could have done

00:06:49.463 --> 00:06:51.364
with that same capital instead.

00:06:52.664 --> 00:06:55.735
Here's how to actually estimate your own probability

00:06:55.735 --> 00:06:59.279
of qualifying, rather than just guessing optimistically.

00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:03.121
Read the protocol's own stated eligibility criteria closely,

00:07:03.121 --> 00:07:06.444
and check your own activity honestly against it.

00:07:06.444 --> 00:07:10.306
Look at how similar, comparable protocols have handled past

00:07:10.306 --> 00:07:14.467
airdrops, for a realistic sense of typical qualifying rates.

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:17.094
And when genuinely unsure, run the math at

00:07:17.094 --> 00:07:20.332
a deliberately conservative guess, not a hopeful one.

00:07:21.632 --> 00:07:24.255
Here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:07:24.255 --> 00:07:26.940
example exists to prove, worth holding onto

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:30.107
more than the specific dollar figures involved.

00:07:30.107 --> 00:07:33.268
In this exact scenario, the farm's expected value

00:07:33.268 --> 00:07:36.934
doesn't even clear the safer alternative's return, before

00:07:36.934 --> 00:07:39.463
its extra risk is even accounted for at

00:07:39.463 --> 00:07:42.181
all, which means the honest answer here is

00:07:42.181 --> 00:07:44.267
that farming is the worse choice.

00:07:45.567 --> 00:07:46.657
Quick check.

00:07:46.657 --> 00:07:50.132
What's the actual formula for an airdrop's expected value?

00:07:54.552 --> 00:07:58.315
The answer: probability of qualifying, times the airdrop's

00:07:58.315 --> 00:08:01.057
value, minus your real costs of farming it.

00:08:02.357 --> 00:08:05.286
Here's why the checklist's first item specifically

00:08:05.286 --> 00:08:08.215
distinguishes these two situations, since the real

00:08:08.215 --> 00:08:11.271
cost of farming differs enormously between them.

00:08:11.271 --> 00:08:14.611
Using a protocol you'd genuinely use anyway means

00:08:14.611 --> 00:08:17.082
the points are simply a bonus on top

00:08:17.082 --> 00:08:20.088
of activity you were already doing, at close

00:08:20.088 --> 00:08:21.991
to zero extra real cost.

00:08:21.991 --> 00:08:25.206
Taking on entirely new actions, purely to farm

00:08:25.206 --> 00:08:28.284
points, means every one of those costs, gas,

00:08:28.284 --> 00:08:30.884
time, and risk, is a real cost you're

00:08:30.884 --> 00:08:34.646
taking on solely for a speculative, unpromised outcome.

00:08:35.946 --> 00:08:37.406
Here's your checklist.

00:08:37.406 --> 00:08:39.831
Do it before farming anything for points.

00:08:39.831 --> 00:08:42.671
Only take actions you'd genuinely take anyway,

00:08:42.671 --> 00:08:45.752
or set a strictly capped, specifically speculative

00:08:45.752 --> 00:08:47.986
budget for it, and nothing more.

00:08:47.986 --> 00:08:50.678
Compare its expected value against what that

00:08:50.678 --> 00:08:53.251
same capital could earn elsewhere, the way

00:08:53.251 --> 00:08:55.226
the worked example just did.

00:08:55.226 --> 00:08:57.486
And only ever claim through links you've

00:08:57.486 --> 00:09:00.241
verified from the protocol's own official channel.

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:02.786
Let's recap.

00:09:02.786 --> 00:09:06.986
Airdrops reward early users, tracked first as points, decided

00:09:06.986 --> 00:09:10.809
by eligibility rules, a snapshot, and sybil filters.

00:09:10.809 --> 00:09:13.319
Nothing here is ever promised, which is

00:09:13.319 --> 00:09:15.891
exactly what fake claim sites exploit at

00:09:15.891 --> 00:09:18.199
the moment of peak anticipation.

00:09:18.199 --> 00:09:21.121
An airdrop farm has a real expected value,

00:09:21.121 --> 00:09:24.383
probability times value minus costs, and it has

00:09:24.383 --> 00:09:27.170
to beat what the same capital could earn

00:09:27.170 --> 00:09:30.324
safely elsewhere, plus its own extra risk.

00:09:30.324 --> 00:09:32.379
Price it as the bet it actually is.

00:09:33.678 --> 00:09:36.067
Only farm what you'd do anyway, or a

00:09:36.067 --> 00:09:40.330
capped speculative budget, compare the opportunity cost honestly,

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.471
and claim only from verified official links.

00:09:43.471 --> 00:09:45.976
Next up, Lesson 4.7: stablecoin

00:09:45.976 --> 00:09:49.421
savings rates and yield-bearing stablecoins.
